\serialprint sends text over the open serial port, as
characters.
It takes one or more arguments, which are sent separated
by a single space, without a newline at the end:

\serialprint hello world

The line above sends the eleven characters of
"hello world".
A serial port must be opened first with \openserialport,
otherwise you will get a "serial port is not open" error.

To send numbers as bytes, use \serialwrite.
